{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"gh pr view 1088 --repo endojs/endo-but-for-bots --json number,url,state,isDraft,headRepositoryOwner,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefName,baseRefOid,title,body,mergeable,statusCheckRollup; gh api repos/kriscendobot/endo-but-for-bots/git/ref/heads/ironhorse-fuzz-findings --jq '.object.sha'; gh api repos/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/commits/38ca1d189384245dd9accfcc2f79763a3b8ec5cb --jq '.sha'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"baseRefName\":\"llm\",\"baseRefOid\":\"38ca1d189384245dd9accfcc2f79763a3b8ec5cb\",\"body\":\"Standing pull request for the continuous **Ironhorse fuzz** campaign: each\\nreproduced finding amends this one PR with a load-bearing regression case and\\nits causal fix. Base `llm`. Adopted by the durable marker below.\\n\\n<!-- garden-job: ironhorse-fuzz-findings -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `493390fc03979205` — target `differential_regexp_surface`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 4-byte minimized input `08 74 74 2a` (sha256\\n`450a95b7…dabbcf7`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into a deeply\\nnested `new RegExp(<pattern>, \\\"s\\\").toString()` whose rendered source is **1099\\nbytes**. The target panicked with a *result* divergence vs the XS pin.\\n\\n**Root cause (oracle-side, not a port defect).** The XS differential oracle\\ncaptured the completion value into a fixed **1024-byte** buffer\\n(`ENDOR_RESULT_MAX`) and `strncpy`-truncated it to 1023 bytes. The port\\n(`ironhorse`) rendered the whole 1099-byte string **correctly**, so the harness\\ncompared the oracle's truncated 1023-byte prefix against the port's full result\\nand reported a false divergence. Any program whose completion value exceeds the\\nbuffer was a latent false positive of this class.\\n\\n**Fix (minimal, oracle-side).**\\n- Grow `ENDOR_RESULT_MAX` to **16 KiB** so realistic completion values compare\\n  in full instead of at a 1 KiB cliff.\\n- Record the untruncated byte length (`result_len`) at both capture sites and\\n  surface `OracleOutcome::result_truncated`. When a value still overflows the\\n  buffer, `differential_check_with_symbols` / `differential_check_result_only`\\n  **skip honestly** (like an out-of-subset program) instead of comparing a\\n  truncated prefix — so the class can never spuriously diverge again, at any\\n  size.\\n\\n**Regressions.**\\n- `ironhorse-fuzz`: replays the exact 4-byte input through the real differential\\n  check and asserts **no divergence** (load-bearing — fails if the oracle fix is\\n  reverted).\\n- `xs-oracle`: a 2000-char completion value is captured **untruncated** and not\\n  flagged truncated.\\n- `ironhorse-vm`: replays the finding's program as frozen bytecode+symbols\\n  (**no XS oracle / no `c/moddable` submodule**) and asserts the port renders\\n  the whole 1099-byte result without panicking or truncating.\\n\\n**Note (latent twin).** `ModuleRunOutcome` shares the same fixed result buffer;\\nthe C side now records `result_len` on that path too, but the module differential\\ntargets do not yet consult a `result_truncated` flag. Out of scope for this\\nfinding; worth a follow-up if a module fuzz target ever surfaces a >16 KiB result.\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `d99d263fcf6ca7a7` — target `differential_source`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 5-byte minimized input `2d 57 27 48 86` (sha256\\n`749f2021…e09a4f9`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into the\\narithmetic program\\n\\n```js\\n((729808896 && (729808896 && (-83 && 327155712)))\\n * (((-83 && 327155712) * (729808896 % 603979776)) % 729808896))\\n```\\n\\nThe target panicked with a *result* divergence:\\n`oracle=\\\"57632001481506816\\\"` vs `ironhorse=\\\"57632001481506820\\\"`.\\n\\n**Root cause (harness-side, not a port defect).** In IEEE-754 doubles\\n(ECMAScript Number semantics) the program evaluates to\\n`327155712 * 176160768` = `819 · 2^46`, the **exactly-representable** double\\nwhose real value is `57632001481506816`. Both engines compute the *identical*\\ndouble — they differ only in how they render it to a string:\\n\\n- XS's `fx_dtoa` (xsdtoa.c) prints the double's exact integer,\\n  `57632001481506816` (17 significant digits).\\n- ironhorse — like V8/SpiderMonkey and ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1.20's Number\\n  `toString` (\\\"let *k* be as small as possible\\\") — prints the **shortest**\\n  round-tripping decimal, `57632001481506820` (16 significant digits).\\n\\nBoth spellings parse back to the same double, so the port was never wrong;\\nironhorse is the spec-conformant one (verified against Node/V8, which prints\\n`57632001481506820`). The harness compared the raw completion strings\\nbyte-for-byte and read XS's non-shortest rendering as a value divergence — the\\nsame *harness-artifact, not engine bug* shape as findings `493390fc03979205`\\nand `5d122a6fc10babd9`.\\n\\n**Fix (minimal, harness-side).** Compare **Number** completions by their\\nIEEE-754 double rather than their decimal spelling. A new `results_agree`\\nhelper treats two result strings as equal when they are byte-identical **or**\\nboth parse as the same finite `f64`; the three differential checks\\n(`differential_check`, `…_with_symbols`, `…_result_only`) call it at their\\nresult-comparison site. Two *different* doubles never share a parse, so every\\ngenuine value divergence is still caught; only differently-spelled renderings\\nof the *same* double are suppressed. Non-numeric completions (booleans,\\nstrings, `Infinity`/`NaN`) fall through to the byte comparison unchanged.\\n\\n**Regressions.**\\n- `ironhorse-fuzz`: replays the exact 5-byte input through the real\\n  `differential_check` and asserts **no divergence** (load-bearing — fails if\\n  the fix is reverted), plus a `results_agree` unit test pinning the\\n  same-double / different-double boundary.\\n- `ironhorse-vm` (`finding_d99d263fcf6ca7a7_large_integer_dtoa`): pins the\\n  port's `number_to_ecma_string` for the reproducing value to the shortest\\n  round-tripping decimal `57632001481506820` (matching V8, not XS's exact\\n  integer). Builds **without the XS oracle or the `c/moddable` submodule**, and\\n  is the crate CI actually runs (`cargo test -p ironhorse-vm`).\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `637d760bc2e0278e` — target `differential_regexp`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 4-byte minimized input `68 d4 80 df` (sha256\\n`8c07999756e8…4e0130`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into a deeply\\nnested, **backreference-heavy** pattern (999-char source, groups `\\\\1`…`\\\\21`)\\nover the subject `\\\"b 00b\\\"` at start offset 0. The pattern never matches, but the\\nport explores **458132** metered backtracking steps before failing, so the raw\\n16.16 match meter is `458132 * 65536 = 30024138752` (> `u32::MAX`). The target\\npanicked with a *match meter* divergence: `ironhorse=30024138752` vs\\n`pin=4254334976`.\\n\\n**Root cause (oracle-side, not a port defect).** `4254334976` is exactly\\n`30024138752 mod 2^32` — the XS differential oracle copied the pin's 64-bit\\n`meterIndex` match meter into a 32-bit `txU4` field, wrapping the true value and\\nmanufacturing a false divergence. The port (`ironhorse_regexp`) meters into a\\n`u64` and reported the correct full-width figure all along. This is the **same\\nroot cause** as the already-fixed findings `5d122a6fc10babd9` /\\n`8275793bca439f6e` / `407764ab1120ed1a` (and the concurrently-landed\\n`5565a021a8cc30bc`): the differential-regexp meter-truncation class.\\n\\n**Fix.** The oracle-side 64-bit meter widening that resolved\\n`5d122a6fc10babd9` already covers this input — verified: at the branch head the\\nexact reproducing case now returns agreement (`differential_check_regexp → Ok`),\\nwhereas at the finding SHA `38ca1d18` it diverged as above. No new code change\\nwas required; the load-bearing deliverable for this finding is the regression\\nlock below, extending coverage of the class to a backreference-driven\\n(rather than empty-match-driven) meter blow-up.\\n\\n**Regression.**\\n- `ironhorse-vm`: `finding_637d760bc2e0278e_regexp_meter_overflow.rs` replays the\\n  byte-exact generated pattern/subject/offset and pins the port's full-width\\n  match meter (`30024138752`) bit-exact, asserting it stays `> u32::MAX` and is\\n  never the 32-bit-wrapped `4254334976`. Builds **without the XS oracle or the\\n  `c/moddable` submodule** — the portable source-of-truth the oracle must agree\\n  with, guarding the matcher against ever silently narrowing the meter.\\n\",\"headRefName\":\"ironhorse-fuzz-findings\",\"headRefOid\":\"92a7cb9cc0b5898cfb30a5211206d0bf18ca9052\",\"headRepositoryOwner\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\"},\"isDraft\":true,\"mergeable\":\"MERGEABLE\",\"number\":1088,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/actions/runs/33353096148/job/99370097827\",\"name\":\"lint\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-31T03:12:53Z\",\"status\":\"IN_PROGRESS\",\"workflowName\":\"CI\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-08-31T03:15:30Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/actions/runs/33353096106/job/99370089358\",\"name\":\"browser-tests\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-31T03:12:50Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"Browser Tests\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-08-31T03:13:05Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/actions/runs/33353096120/job/99370050693\",\"name\":\"build\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-31T03:12:35Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"Test project mutual dependency versions\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-08-31T03:12:47Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/actions/runs/33353096048/job/99370050348\",\"name\":\"zizmor\",\"startedA